  "title": "🌃 Berlin by Night: An Insightful Walk Tour",
  "date": "2025-11-10T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "🌃 Berlin by Night: An Insightful Walk Tour\n\nWe arrived at the Berlin Hauptbahnhof (Main Station) at night. What an impressive structure! 🤯 It's incredibly vast, with trains stopping on different levels, truly giving an \"Inception-like\" feeling.\n\nDB\n\nA Cold Welcome? The Cash Conundrum 🪙\n\nSurprisingly, there was no prominent \"Berlin welcomes you\" sign for visitors' photos outside. I sometimes find Berlin a bit \"cold\" in its practicality. We had to use cash to buy train tickets—one route was €9.10! Having to count out so many coins because there was no POS device for card payment, or even a simple QR code for scanning, was a hassle.\n\nDB\n\nFuturistic Visions and Somber History 🚶\n\nThe area around the station is under construction (a common sight in a growing city!). We walked past Futurium (https://futurium.de/en), a fascinating museum dedicated to futuristic environment and living designs.\n\nFuturium\n\nCrossing a river, we came upon a powerful, yet somber, spot: a memorial dedicated to the minorities who died during WWII. A stark reminder of history etched into the city's landscape.\n\nMemorials for minorities in WWII\n\nThe Heart of German Politics 🇩🇪\n\nNext, we passed the German Parliament (Reichstag Building). I learned there are over 600 seats in the German Parliament—it's a massive legislative body!\n\nParliament\n\nFrom Wall to Gate: A Divided Past 🧱\n\nAfter touring the \"western Berlin\" view, we arrived at the iconic Brandenburg Gate. Look down, and you can see a line of shining bricks on the ground—these mark where the infamous Berlin Wall once stood.\n\nThe Berlin Wall\n\nThe famous Unter den Linden road is a wide, straight artery designed to allow tanks to travel across Berlin. The massive Victory Column states its dominance in what was formerly West Berlin.\n\nThe road\n\nBrandenburg gate\n\nThe Dual City: Legacies of the Split 🏙️\n\nIt was fascinating to see Humboldt University of Berlin. I realized that, due to the decades of division, Berlin still maintains a dual-system style in many aspects. There are two major Berlin Theatres, and even two of some schools! This organizational redundancy is a direct, enduring legacy of the Wall, thankfully supported by the huge economic scale of the reunited city.\n\nHumboldt University: school\n\nFrederick the Great: statue\n\nBerlin Theatre: theatre\n\nNew Watchhouse: New Watchhouse\n\nBerlin Cathedral: church\n\nEast Side Vibrancy and Culinary Rewards 🍻\n\nWe finished the walk near Alexanderplatz, which has a different, more vibrant energy.\n\nGraffiti Culture: Graffiti\n\nTransit Layers: Seeing the train on the bridge with a tram running underneath it gave me a fleeting, multi-layered \"Thailand feeling\"! train and tram\n\nFinally, time for a well-deserved meal! We found a restaurant providing Munich (München) food and music.\n\nRestaurant near Alexandar Platz\n\nI finally ate the famous Schweinshaxe (pork knuckle)! It was €20, and the Radler was €6.80. A bit expensive, perhaps, but absolutely delicious! 🤤 The potatoes were soft and wonderfully chewy, almost like small savory breads.\n\nSchweinshaxe\n\nA Delicious Conclusion! 🐷\n\nFunnily enough, I recently posted an AI-generated photo of myself carrying a pig while running... so this meal felt like the perfect \"after\" picture! 😅\n\nMeme",
